#!/usr/bin/env python3
# conveyor-check — run a task file's checklist verifies and tick only what proves.
#   conveyor-check <taskfile>
#
# Grammar, one step per line (PLAN.md Slice 1):
#   - [ ] <claim> :: verify: <command>
#   - [ ] <claim> :: verify: <command> :: expect: <regex>
# Tick = command exits 0 AND expect (if given) matches its output.
# `verify: HUMAN` is never auto-ticked — a judgment step cannot be scripted
# into green — and a hand-tick on one is never revoked by THIS mode (the
# --erase mode below is what reverts one, and the runner calls it after every
# seat leg, before this mode runs).
#
# Non-HUMAN steps are ALWAYS re-verified, ticked or not, and a [x] whose
# command now fails is unticked: the commands are the gate, not the mark —
# otherwise a leg could game the loop by writing [x] directly.
#
# Lines without " :: verify: " are not steps and are left byte-identical.
# No model, no network beyond what the verifies themselves do. Exits nonzero
# if any non-HUMAN step ends unticked; exit 2 = usage/IO error.
#
#   conveyor-check --list <taskfile>
# prints "[x] <claim>" / "[ ] <claim>", one per step, and RUNS NOTHING. Added
# for slice 4's progress rule: the runner needs the checklist's shape (which
# steps exist, which are ticked) before and after a leg, and a second parser
# of the same grammar is how the two would drift apart. One grammar, one
# regex, two readers. Always exits 0 when the file is readable -- --list is a
# question about the file, not about the world.
#
#   conveyor-check --list-json <taskfile>
# same question, machine-readable: one JSON object per step,
# {"ticked": bool, "human": bool, "claim": str}. Added 2026-09-01 for slice
# 5's watcher, which has to tell an unticked HUMAN step (waiting on the operator,
# expected) from an unticked non-HUMAN step in done/ (a defect). --list
# cannot answer that -- it prints the tick and the claim only -- and the
# runner parses --list output positionally, so widening it would break the
# baseline comparison. A THIRD reader of this grammar is the thing the --list
# comment exists to prevent, so the watcher gets a mode here instead. Runs
# nothing; exits 0 when the file is readable.
#
#   conveyor-check --erase <taskfile> <pre-leg-snapshot>
# A SEAT'S TICK CANNOT MATTER (2026-09-04). Restores every step's tick to the
# state it had in <pre-leg-snapshot> (a copy of the task file taken before the
# seat ran — the runner's logs/<slug>.prebump) and leaves any step that did not
# exist there unticked. The runner calls this after EVERY seat leg and BEFORE
# the check mode, so the only ticks a task file ever carries are this
# checker's (command steps) and conveyor-tap's (HUMAN steps). Why the runner
# needs it when the check mode already re-verifies every command step: a
# hand-tick on a command step was harmless to ROUTING but survived in the file
# for the verifier seat to read and fail the task on process (cash-gap,
# 2026-09-04 20:41 — a build leg and a verify leg spent on a mark the code had
# already made irrelevant); and a hand-tick on a HUMAN step was never revoked
# at all, so a driver writing `[x]` on `the operator confirms this spec :: verify:
# HUMAN` forged the human gate and the runner routed on a ruling the operator never
# made. Only conveyor-tap sets a HUMAN tick, and it does so BETWEEN legs, so
# a HUMAN tick that appeared during a leg is a forgery and one that vanished
# during a leg is the operator's ruling being erased — both are reverted to the
# snapshot.
# RESTORE-TO-SNAPSHOT, NOT UNTICK-ALL, on purpose: the task that queued this
# said "every non-HUMAN step is unticked", but the verifier-fail path and
# every dead-end path run NO check mode afterwards, and the runner's progress
# rule (made_progress) reads the ticks in the file — unticking every command
# step there would make a moving task read as a zero-progress round trip and
# dead-end it. Wherever the check mode does follow, the two are the same
# end state, since it re-verifies every command step regardless.
# THE SIBLING BYPASS, closed 2026-09-05: this mode also RESTORES A STEP THE
# SEAT REMOVED. Rewriting the tick character of steps that are still present
# closes the forged-tick route and leaves the DELETION route wide open, and the
# two are equally effective — a seat whose only change is
# `sed -i '/:: verify: HUMAN$/d'` used to send the task to verify/ with the gate
# simply gone, no intent minted, the operator never asked (verified against the live
# runner, 2026-09-04, by the verifier leg that refused this task). made_progress
# iterates the CURRENT steps, so a removed step was invisible to it too. Every
# step in the snapshot that is not in the file is inserted back whole, at its
# snapshot position, carrying its snapshot tick — so a HUMAN gate comes back
# unticked and parks the task on the operator, and a gate conveyor-tap had already
# ruled on comes back ticked. If the seat deleted the `## Checklist` heading
# along with every step, that heading is restored too: without it the next leg
# reads the task as having no checklist and spends a SPEC seat on a task that
# was mid-build.
# KEYED BY CLAIM, like the tick pass and like made_progress. Editing a step's
# verify COMMAND passes through untouched — EXCEPT a HUMAN step's, whose kind is
# pinned (see the third route below). 2026-09-04's step-7 repair was a
# command-to-command edit and is legitimate; renaming a CLAIM reads as a delete
# plus an add, so the original comes back and the reworded one stays. That is
# the intended direction — a step is never silently retired, and the driver is
# told in executor-preamble.md that the checklist is append-only.
# THE THIRD AND FOURTH ROUTES, closed by pinning a step's KIND in BOTH
# directions: rewrite the `verify:` and leave the claim alone, and the tick pass
# recognises the step while the deletion pass never misses it. DOWNGRADE,
# `:: verify: HUMAN` → `:: verify: true` — the tick pass unticks it, then the
# checker runs `true` and ticks it, and the task files itself with the operator never
# asked. UPGRADE, an already-ticked command step → `:: verify: HUMAN`
# (2026-09-05) — the tick already MATCHES the snapshot, so nothing was reverted
# and nothing printed; the checker never re-ticks or revokes a HUMAN step and
# route_done greps only for an UNTICKED gate, so the task reached done/ with a
# ticked HUMAN box no tap ever set, and the command that proved that step
# stopped running. A step whose kind differs from the snapshot's is reinstated
# whole, either way; a step ABSENT from the snapshot is left alone, so appending
# a real HUMAN gate still works.
# LEFT OPEN, deliberately: a seat that WEAKENS a command step's verify
# (`bash tests/x.sh` → `true`). Nothing in this file can tell that from the
# step-7 repair, and refusing both would block the repairs; the verifier seat
# reads the diff and is the right instrument for it.
# Prints one line per reverted step — `ERASED command <claim>` (a hand-tick),
# `ERASED HUMAN <claim>` (a forgery), `RESTORED command|HUMAN <claim>` (a tick
# the seat removed), `RESTORED_KIND command|HUMAN <claim>` (a step whose kind
# the seat rewrote — the kind named is the one REINSTATED, so a downgraded gate
# reads HUMAN and an upgraded command step reads command),
# `RESTORED_MISSING command|HUMAN <claim>` (a whole step the
# seat deleted), `RESTORED_MISSING section ## Checklist` — and nothing when the
# seat left the checklist alone. Runs no commands; only ticks, rewritten
# `verify:` kinds and deleted lines change, every other byte is preserved;
# exit 2 = IO.
# A THIRD parser of the grammar is the thing the --list comment exists to
# prevent, which is why this is a mode here and not a sed in the runner.
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

STEP_RE = re.compile(r"^(\s*- \[)( |x)(\] )(.*?) :: verify: (.*)$")
TIMEOUT = int(os.environ.get("CONVEYOR_CHECK_TIMEOUT", "120"))


# TASK FILES IN AND OUT (2026-10-26). Every task has an output folder and may
# have attachments. conveyor-add writes "outputs: <abs path>" under
# "## Outputs"; a task without that line (older tasks, other writers) gets
# <belt root>/outputs/<task file stem>. Attachments live in the sibling
# attachments/<same id>/. Check mode exports these to every verify command as
# $CONVEYOR_OUTPUTS, $CONVEYOR_ATTACHMENTS and $CONVEYOR_TASK_ID, so a step
# can say `test -s "$CONVEYOR_OUTPUTS/report.csv"`. `--io <taskfile>` prints
# the same three as KEY=VALUE lines for the runner: one reader, not two.
OUTPUTS_RE = re.compile(r"^outputs: (/.*?)\s*$")


def task_io(path, lines):
    outputs = None
    for line in lines:
        m = OUTPUTS_RE.match(line.rstrip("\n"))
        if m:
            outputs = m.group(1).rstrip("/")
            break
    if outputs is None:
        root = os.environ.get("CONVEYOR_ROOT") or os.path.dirname(
            os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(path)))
        stem = os.path.splitext(os.path.basename(path))[0]
        outputs = os.path.join(os.path.abspath(root), "outputs", stem)
    task_id = os.path.basename(outputs)
    belt = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(outputs))
    return {"CONVEYOR_TASK_ID": task_id,
            "CONVEYOR_OUTPUTS": outputs,
            "CONVEYOR_ATTACHMENTS": os.path.join(belt, "attachments", task_id)}


def split_expect(rest):
    """Command, then optional regex after the LAST ' :: expect: '."""
    idx = rest.rfind(" :: expect: ")
    if idx == -1:
        return rest.strip(), None
    return rest[:idx].strip(), rest[idx + len(" :: expect: "):].strip()


def failure_body(stdout, stderr):
    """The part of a failed check worth showing again. The traceback, or the tail.
    The head of a long log is what gets dropped."""
    stdout = stdout or ""
    stderr = stderr or ""
    marker = "Traceback (most recent call last):"
    if stderr.strip() and marker not in stderr and marker in stdout:
        body = stdout
    else:
        body = stderr if stderr.strip() else stdout
    body = body.replace("\x00", "")
    start = body.rfind(marker)
    if start != -1:
        chunk = body[start:]
    else:
        chunk = "\n".join(body.splitlines()[-40:])
    chunk = chunk.strip("\n")
    if len(chunk) > 2400:
        chunk = chunk[-2400:].lstrip("\n")
    return chunk.strip()


def run_step(command, expect):
    """True iff the command exits 0 and expect (if any) matches its output."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            command, shell=True, executable="/bin/bash",
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=TIMEOUT,
        )
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as err:
        body = failure_body(getattr(err, "stdout", None), getattr(err, "stderr", None))
        note = "timeout after %ds" % TIMEOUT
        return False, note + ("\n" + body if body else "")
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        body = failure_body(proc.stdout, proc.stderr)
        return False, "exit %d%s" % (proc.returncode, ("\n" + body) if body else "")
    if expect is not None:
        try:
            # rstrip the trailing newline before matching. Without it, MULTILINE
            # gives every command output a phantom empty line at the end, so
            # `expect: ^$` (the natural way to write "no output") matched ANY
            # output and could never fail — a check that cannot fail, inside the
            # thing whose job is to falsify. Found 2026-09-01 when a step
            # asserting a clean `git status --porcelain` went green while the
            # file was untracked. Truly empty output still matches `^$`, which
            # is the intended meaning.
            out = (proc.stdout + proc.stderr).rstrip("\n")
            if not re.search(expect, out, re.MULTILINE):
                body = failure_body(proc.stdout, proc.stderr)
                return False, "expect %r not matched%s" % (expect, ("\n" + body) if body else "")
        except re.error as e:
            return False, "bad expect regex: %s" % e
    return True, "exit 0"


def step_kind(rest):
    """HUMAN or command, from a step's post-`verify:` remainder."""
    command, _ = split_expect(rest)
    return "HUMAN" if command == "HUMAN" else "command"


def erase_hand_ticks(path, snapshot):
    """--erase: every step's tick back to the snapshot's; new steps unticked;
    every step the seat deleted restored whole, at its snapshot position."""
    try:
        with open(snapshot) as f:
            snap_lines = f.readlines()
        with open(path) as f:
            lines = f.readlines()
    except OSError as e:
        print("conveyor-check: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    # Keyed by claim, like the runner's progress rule (made_progress): a step
    # the seat reworded is a step that did not exist before the leg.
    before = {}              # claim -> (tick, kind, whole snapshot line)
    snap_steps = []          # (claim, line) in snapshot order
    for line in snap_lines:
        m = STEP_RE.match(line.rstrip("\n"))
        if m:
            before[m.group(4)] = (m.group(2), step_kind(m.group(5)), line)
            snap_steps.append((m.group(4), line))
    changed = False
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        m = STEP_RE.match(line.rstrip("\n"))
        if not m:
            continue
        prefix, tick, close, claim, rest = m.groups()
        want, want_kind, want_line = before.get(claim, (" ", None, None))
        kind = step_kind(rest)
        # A STEP'S KIND IS PINNED, BOTH WAYS. Two routes to the same forgery
        # ride on rewriting `verify:` and leaving the CLAIM alone, so the tick
        # pass recognises the step and the deletion pass never misses it.
        #   THIRD ROUTE — DOWNGRADE, `:: verify: HUMAN` → `:: verify: true`:
        # the tick pass restores `[ ]`, the checker then runs `true`, ticks it,
        # and the task files itself with no intent minted.
        #   FOURTH ROUTE — UPGRADE, an already-ticked command step rewritten to
        # `:: verify: HUMAN` (closed 2026-09-05, refuted by the verifier leg
        # that had just passed the third). The tick MATCHES the snapshot, so the
        # tick pass fell through its `tick == want` continue and printed
        # nothing; the checker never re-ticks or revokes a HUMAN step, and
        # route_done greps only for an UNTICKED HUMAN gate — so the task walked
        # to verify/ and then done/ carrying a ticked HUMAN box with no tap, no
        # `## Leg N — HUMAN ruling` block and no receipt, while the command that
        # used to prove that step was never run again by any later leg. A ticked
        # command step sits in the snapshot of every bounced task, so the route
        # is not hypothetical.
        # Neither direction is ever legitimate: nothing turns a judgment step
        # into a scripted one, and a seat that needs a NEW human gate appends
        # one — a step absent from the snapshot is left alone (and unticked by
        # the tick pass), so appending still works. So a kind mismatch either
        # way puts the whole snapshot line back, tick included: that tick is the
        # snapshot's, never this seat's.
        # command → command edits still pass through untouched — that is the
        # step-7 repair (2026-09-04), and a seat that WEAKENS a command
        # (`bash tests/x.sh` → `true`) is deliberately left to the verifier
        # seat: no rule here can tell that from a legitimate repair, and
        # guessing would block the repairs.
        if want_kind is not None and want_kind != kind:
            print("RESTORED_KIND %s %s" % (want_kind, claim))
            lines[i] = want_line if want_line.endswith("\n") else want_line + "\n"
            changed = True
            continue
        if tick == want:
            continue
        print("%s %s %s" % ("ERASED" if tick == "x" else "RESTORED", kind, claim))
        nl = "\n" if line.endswith("\n") else ""
        lines[i] = prefix + want + close + claim + " :: verify: " + rest + nl
        changed = True

    # --- the deletion route ---------------------------------------------------
    # Indices are re-derived after every insert rather than batched: a seat that
    # both deleted AND reordered steps makes a precomputed anchor list stale,
    # and a task file is ~100 lines, so the rescan costs nothing.
    def step_index(claim):
        for i, ln in enumerate(lines):
            m = STEP_RE.match(ln.rstrip("\n"))
            if m and m.group(4) == claim:
                return i
        return None

    def first_step_index():
        for i, ln in enumerate(lines):
            if STEP_RE.match(ln.rstrip("\n")):
                return i
        return None

    def has_heading(where):
        return any(ln.startswith("## Checklist") for ln in where)

    missing = [s for s in snap_steps if step_index(s[0]) is None]
    if missing and has_heading(snap_lines) and not has_heading(lines):
        # The whole block went. Restore the heading first so the steps below
        # land under it and the next leg does not read this as a spec task.
        if lines and not lines[-1].endswith("\n"):
            lines[-1] += "\n"
        lines.append("\n## Checklist\n")
        print("RESTORED_MISSING section ## Checklist")
        changed = True
    anchor = None            # line index of the last snapshot step still present
    for claim, snap_line in snap_steps:
        idx = step_index(claim)
        if idx is not None:
            anchor = idx
            continue
        if anchor is not None:
            pos = anchor + 1                     # back in its snapshot order
        else:
            nxt = first_step_index()             # nothing survived before it
            pos = nxt if nxt is not None else len(lines)
        if not snap_line.endswith("\n"):
            snap_line += "\n"
        lines.insert(pos, snap_line)
        anchor = pos
        m = STEP_RE.match(snap_line.rstrip("\n"))
        print("RESTORED_MISSING %s %s" % (step_kind(m.group(5)), claim))
        changed = True

    if changed:
        try:
            with open(path, "w") as f:
                f.write("".join(lines))
        except OSError as e:
            print("conveyor-check: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    return 0


def main():
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    list_only = json_only = erase = io_only = False
    if args and args[0] == "--io":
        io_only = True
        args = args[1:]
    elif args and args[0] == "--list":
        list_only = True
        args = args[1:]
    elif args and args[0] == "--list-json":
        json_only = True
        args = args[1:]
    elif args and args[0] == "--erase":
        erase = True
        args = args[1:]
    if len(args) != (2 if erase else 1):
        print("usage: conveyor-check [--list|--list-json] <taskfile>\n"
              "       conveyor-check --erase <taskfile> <pre-leg-snapshot>\n"
              "       conveyor-check --io <taskfile>",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    path = args[0]
    if erase:
        return erase_hand_ticks(path, args[1])
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            lines = f.readlines()
    except OSError as e:
        print("conveyor-check: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    old_content = "".join(lines)
    io = task_io(path, lines)
    if io_only:
        for k in ("CONVEYOR_TASK_ID", "CONVEYOR_OUTPUTS", "CONVEYOR_ATTACHMENTS"):
            print("%s=%s" % (k, io[k]))
        return 0

    if list_only or json_only:
        for line in lines:
            m = STEP_RE.match(line.rstrip("\n"))
            if not m:
                continue
            if list_only:
                print("[%s] %s" % (m.group(2), m.group(4)))
            else:
                command, _ = split_expect(m.group(5))
                print(json.dumps({"ticked": m.group(2) == "x",
                                  "human": command == "HUMAN",
                                  "claim": m.group(4)},
                                 separators=(",", ":")))
        return 0

    os.environ.update(io)  # every verify command below inherits these
    steps = unticked = 0
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        m = STEP_RE.match(line.rstrip("\n"))
        if not m:
            continue
        steps += 1
        prefix, tick, close, claim, rest = m.groups()
        command, expect = split_expect(rest)

        if command == "HUMAN":
            print("[%s] HUMAN %s" % (tick, claim))
            continue

        ok, why = run_step(command, expect)
        new_tick = "x" if ok else " "
        if new_tick != tick:
            nl = "\n" if line.endswith("\n") else ""
            lines[i] = prefix + new_tick + close + claim + " :: verify: " + rest + nl
        if ok:
            print("[x] PASS  %s" % claim)
        else:
            unticked += 1
            print("[ ] FAIL  %s (%s)" % (claim, why))

    if steps == 0:
        print("conveyor-check: no checklist steps found")
        return 0

    new_content = "".join(lines)
    if new_content != old_content:
        with open(path, "w") as f:
            f.write(new_content)
    return 1 if unticked else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
